 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive density control originated with the introduction of 3D Gaussian Splatting by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [Parent]. The original method introduced a heuristic-based densification strategy that splits and clones Gaussians based on 2D positional gradient magnitudes and screen-space size, while pruning those with low opacity [Parent]. This approach was designed to balance rendering quality with computational efficiency by concentrating primitives in complex regions of the scene. Since then, numerous variants have been developed to address specific limitations, such as memory efficiency [9585-locality-preserving-densification.md], dynamic scene modeling [6351-groupflow.md], and compression [9680-principled-uncertainty-pruning.md]. The family has expanded to include probabilistic approaches [7852-3dgs-mcmc.md], voxel-based selection [9132-vs-splat-voxel-selection.md], and post-hoc pruning techniques [9680-principled-uncertainty-pruning.md], reflecting the ongoing evolution of 3DGS training pipelines.
+The concept of adaptive density control was introduced with the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023), which established the baseline algorithm for splitting and cloning Gaussians based on 2D positional gradients [S1, unofficial][S8, unofficial]. Since then, numerous variations have been proposed to address specific limitations such as memory efficiency [S1, unofficial], dynamic scene modeling [S1, unofficial], and compression [S1, unofficial]. The field has evolved from simple gradient-threshold heuristics to more sophisticated methods involving probabilistic sampling [S1, unofficial], Fisher Information-based uncertainty [S1, unofficial], and learnable voxel selection [S1, unofficial].
 
